    "turn_text": "Welcome back to the post-industrial podcast. I'm your host, Carmen Gentile. We've been away for a while. I think more than two months prior to the election. So yes, clearly more than two months. The reason being that events were moving so quickly, we felt like we couldn't keep pace with a weekly podcast. So we waited for some, I guess what we consider to be daybreak or daylight or clarity, in their results and look what we got for our patients. A nation on the brink of civil war. I'm just going to say it. With numerous attacks being planned on the capital and capitals throughout post-industrial America and the entire country, we are in a worse place as a nation that at any time in my 46 years. I say that without a hint of hyperbole. We have a leadership crisis in this country, the likes of which hasn't befallen us since the Civil War. We need help. We need help from the rational among the elected to convince those that led their constituents astray that they need to ratchet down the violence, the hate, the delusion. Although handling that last one is going to be hard. I don't know even where to begin to undilude the millions of Americans who have been led down a dark path by Donald Trump. But there are those out there who are willing and able to lead us out of this nightmare. And I think one of those folks is my next guest. Lieutenant Governor John Federman has gained national prominence of late for his outspoken defense of democracy and repudiation of his Republican colleagues both in Pennsylvania and on Capitol Hill. He is now poised to potentially make a run for the Senate in 2022, having raised half a million dollars in less than a day for his exploratory committee. My post-industrial partner, Kim Palmero and I, just spoke to the governor about what's happening on Capitol Hill, what's happening at the local level in America that has ignited this hatred and resentment and delusion. And we also talk about legalizing weed in PA. Take a listen. Lieutenant Governor Federman, thank you very much for joining us on the Post Industrial Podcast. My pleasure. Thank you for having me. Great, so I know you're pressed for time, so I'm just gonna jump right into it. We are at a crossroads in America right now. In my estimation, this is in my 46 years of being around, this is the greatest crisis of confidence, crisis of faith, crisis of unity that the nation has ever experienced in the last half century. A lot of this angst that starts at the local level with frustrations people have about their home lives, their work situation, their quality of life that's diminished over decades, and has manifest on the national level. As someone who has national prominence now, but who started their public service career at the local level, what can you tell us about that frustration that folks may be experiencing on the local level? Well, the frustration and the anger and the despair and the heartbreak on the ground is authentic, but the messaging at the highest level with the president and others in his orbit is incredibly synthetic. They try to tap into that. They take what's real and then they turn it into something synthetic and divisive, which is quite frankly reprehensible. And it's one thing, much of politics is professional wrestling, at least it used to be, where it was understood. You get a folding chair to the back, but it's not full speed or it's not personal. It's this idea that there's some things that are naturally inherently conflicting. It's a much darker and much more sinister turn where the enemy is now been made to be each other. And you have a deadly pandemic that's going to kill at least 400,000 Americans, maybe even half a million before it's all done. And that should be the enemy. And still, instead, excuse me, we're arguing about whether we should wear a mask. Or whether we should have an indoor wedding with a hundred guests or you name it and things that would be common sense or not even just common sense would be for the greater good people would be falling over themselves to embrace these simple moves. Now we're arguing over them and we're swearing up over the dumbest things. ever, you know? We wouldn't square up on whether or not you should flush your toilet. We wouldn't square up on whether or not you should walk in the middle of a busy street. You know, these things are just common sense. But now we have, and you know, there's tens of thousands if not more people dead because of it. But I'm not a pessimist. I'm actually optimistic. Our democracy received this significant test over these last two months. Pennsylvania was at the epicenter of a lot of that. Our electoral integrity was attacked daily, routinely. and it held and we delivered a true result that I've always said we would deliver and it was we had three cases of voter fraud now one single documented instance otherwise and I've been challenged anyone. But all of it held. It all held. And Joe Biden is going to be sworn in as president in less than a week, or in around a little over a week, excuse me. And I think that's a tribute to the guardrails. It's a tribute to the ethical members of the other side. We have them in our state. There's those across the country that did not go along and down this awful, seditious road. That so many other people were willing to for whatever reason out of fear for going against their party or even works that they sought to exploit it for their own political game like a Ted Cruz or a josh holly who know better than me they're both ideally graduates they know there was no voter fraud they know it's statistically insignificant thing and yet they traded. damaging the democratic franchise of our nation, hoping to curry favor and gain some kind of electoral advantage for 2024? Well, as it pertains to elected officials, be they in Pennsylvania that you deal with a legislature or on Capitol Hill, Those that capitalize on that insecurity or what we would call a post-factual era in American politics, to me, that is particularly reprehensible, as you mentioned. These are guys, you mentioned Hawley and Cruz in particular, who are well-educated and know better. I mean, Cruz of all people, he clerked on the Supreme Court, so he definitely knows the law. But how is it that in your estimation, again, drawing on your experience at the local level now, at the national level? How is it that so many people are susceptible to their lies? Because someone like Ted Cruz doesn't strike me as someone who's particularly charismatic. And for all Trump sins, he was charismatic to a certain number of people. But how, even those that aren't particularly charismatic, and I don't need to call them out by name, still seem to be able to to capitalize on those lies. Sure. And add to them. How is that? Well, I think I just want to be clear. The majority of Republicans know that they lost that election. They do. Oh, I agree. Yes, they do. Yeah. And but yeah, there's a there's a segment of unreachable and whether that's with the truth or whether that's with, you know, like if you on a personal level approached the most zealous of them all and they said, well, you want to bet your house. You want to bet? I mean, no one would take a serious bet. The problem is that there's no penalty for claiming the most awful things about our democracy. And that's what I said consistently through this, is that this is not free speech. You can say Ted Cruz is the worst. Or John Betterman is the worst. That's free speech. You can say that all day. But to say one and one is three is a lie. We know that's not true. To say that there was a rigged election when there was non-no and zero at all, documented at all. And 58 court cases later, not one, Judge said this, there's any merit to any of this. You don't have the right to say that any more than you have a right to yell fire on in a crowded theater when there is no. That is not free speech. And now they've channeled their indignation to social media platforms. You know, it's like social media. I blame a lot of it. You asked that question, well, social media is a big part of it. You know, it is not enough to slap a label on a tweet saying, this is in dispute. And I said this, it's no, it's not in dispute. A dispute is saying, sheets is better than wall law or the Steelers is better than, are better than the Eagles. That's a dispute. What's not in dispute is that the election was fair and free and true. you know what's not in dispute is the law of gravity that one plus one equals two those things are not a dispute either you have someone who affirms that or who lies about it and the president did just that and you saw it hit to a flashpoint because they were allowed he was allowed to You know, hundreds and hundreds of times to lie about something instead of being shut down. The second one of those garbage tweets went up. It should have been taken down immediately, immediately. You know, that is not free speech. That is never can be free speech. And if it becomes free speech, you're going to have another scene like you did in Washington. Right. You mentioned leadership using and weaponizing lies to influence people. You know, before we started our conversation, for a moment there, I saw your wife on camera, Giselle, and I wanted to ask you, and I have to admit, this comes from a very personal place because I used to live in Brazil. I lived in Rio de Janeiro. I was a reporter there for many years. And I was wondering whether her experience having come from Brazil, I know she left when she was very young, but she was alive during the dictatorship there. And she also had come from a part of Rio de Janeiro that was very violent, very troubled. I was wondering whether or not her experience and what she's related of it to you has influenced your perception of maybe what's happening in America, what's happening to people's perceptions and how leaders who are very charismatic, like the current leader of Brazil, Jair Bolsonaro, can influence people to often vote against their own interests. I've heard her, I'm not speaking for my wife, but what she said is there's such a strong parallel between the current leadership in the United States and Brazil. Whether you want to call him the Brazilian Trump or Trump the American version of him. I mean, it's no accident that you have the divisive rhetoric, you have these lies, you have the propaganda, and you have skyrocketing COVID cases and deaths and tragedy stacking on top of tragedy. And this idea that Donald Trump identifies with or understands, even likes his supporters, he would never want to be Duck, quote unquote, living like many of his supporters. It's called the personality. And I don't know how else to explain it. And never before in my life would I ever think that I would see shadow news stations, unquote, that are pure state propaganda, like it's reminiscent of the Soviet Union. I used to look at OAN or Newsmax. It's like Fox News has a perspective and it's understood that it's got a conservative perspective just the way say MSNBC has a perspective and you know, but you have It's it's a virtual reality. It's an alternative reality where where there's a relationship or an understanding that we are not going to tell you anything true and you must want that or you are participating in that because it's scary. you're able to customize your own bubble. And I'm sure you can do the same thing in Brazil, where it's like, you can create the messaging that reinforces whatever beliefs that you have. And it's just become so destructive and so outrageous. And the simple fact of the matter is, is that we have to stop this idea. Like, I'm an absolutist with free speech, absolutist. That's not what free speech is. It's not. It is not free speech. And when you allow lies to be called disputes, you are actually undermining the concept. It's dangerous to free speech. Donald Trump is not entitled to a platform. Wherever that is, to law, he's 