ays, look, we got to take down this virus. Look, you want to, you know what I'm really worried about? The COVID pandemic to me is like a giant flood. And it's only after the water recedes, you know, how much damage truly was done during the flood. And I think once we bring the virus to a heel, we are going to be horrified with the damage that is left in its wake. And that's going to require a national focus that I think Joe Biden is uniquely qualified and positioned to bring to the United States. And I would hope it would be viewed as intensely un-American to stand in the way of rebuilding our country back to the way it was. before this pandemic hit and then stacking the president's handling of it on top of it. And that, I don't say that at the partisan or as a Democrat. I say that as a father of three young children. I say that as a family that has lost somebody that was dearly loved to COVID. I say this as a citizen who wants to ignore my president on a daily basis and not wonder what he said on Twitter and what kind of reactions it's going to be in the world or domestically. I think people want to return to normalcy? Well, in your estimation, you mentioned leadership is one way, but leadership in my estimation has to work hand in hand with better education too. And that's why I mentioned civics and history and people having a basic understanding of how their government works because I feel as though a lot of folks don't have a firm understanding of just what the three branches of government are or whether they're They don't know that there are three different branches of government. We have a senator from Alabama right now, Tommy Tuberville, who didn't know that. You'll play stupid games, win stupid prizes. Alabama did that. You know, they almost elected, I forget his name, that awful guy that was paying for. Way more. You have a guy that has no business being in the Senate. So it's like, and the people of Alabama are going to pay for that. more so than they already have with all their bad choices. I mean you look at you know what state's been on the wrong side of history more than others you know than Alabama, you'd be hard pressed to find one that is more so. And they just won the national championship but they just finished dead last and who they just appointed to be their representative and in Washington. So no one's going to accuse him of Churchillian thinking and the moment of elevating civics and ideals of governance to a new level. But this is also the era that we live in. And I also think that what your average person may not care or know about civics should be supplanted with, you know what? I'm pretty good at figuring out who's trying to divide us, who's trying to exploit our differences versus who's trying to say, look, we don't have to agree, we can work together, we can figure out how to make all of us better off. Well, speaking of the Senate, your Senate exploratory committee has had a successful fundraising effort so far. Does that mean that you're officially throwing your hat in the ring for the Senate? No, we're going to have a decision forthcoming in several weeks, I would imagine. Things are going incredibly well, and I'm truly humbled and grateful for the response that we've received, continued to receive, and I just couldn't be any more grateful, and it's affirming to have that many people step up, and really all I did was tweet an article that just said, yeah, I'm picking a few tires for the Senate in 22. Jim, do you have something you want to talk about? Yeah, I wanted to ask about recreational cannabis. What do you think the path forward is for recreational cannabis in Pennsylvania? What is the immediate future? Well, I refer to it as legal weed because that's what people, you talk about people understanding. It's like, I specifically choose the term legal weed because I'll call it cannabis when people go to their dealer saying, Hi, I'd like to buy some more cannabis off. So I think messaging is important. I think that's critical. So the path to legal weed in Pennsylvania is going to be the minute the Republicans either admit that I was right about legal weed and we need the revenue, the justice, the jobs, the freedom, the help for our farmers, help for our veterans. You know, prohibition is so much harder than just admitting that you have to evolve on weed. But in the absence of that reality is going to crash in on us. New Jersey is going to legalize it. Governor Cuomo just mentioned that that's one of the centerpieces of his agenda. I mean, we are being closed in on by legal weed. And I think Pennsylvania deserves to reap the benefits of legal weed. It's a plant. I can't think of a more bizarre superstition to criminalize a plant with no known medical overdose deaths that you could grow next to your tomatoes. And we have somehow allowed ourselves to create this superstition that it must be controlled, it must be illegal, and we must ruin people's criminal records for the rest of their lives if they are caught with it. It's absurd, it's destructive, and it needs to end. Well, how is that going to resonate in the legislature in 2021 in Pennsylvania? No, I'm going to keep hammering away because I know it's true. I know it's true. This isn't a thing of, well, well, I know it's true. Legal weed is inevitable in Pennsylvania. It's inevitable in the United States. It never should have been illegal for the last 80 years. They legalized And the world didn't spin off its axis. You didn't have stoners, you know, going into field and stealing hemp. You know, I use CBD and it's a remarkable substance. Like the fact that that was denied my entire adult life, you know, and, you know, we push people towards pain pills and all these other awful things. It's an outrage. It's a literal outrage. Oh, I agree. To be honest, I have a prescription for medical marijuana, both because of my eye and because of some of the things I've experienced covering the wars in Afghanistan and Iraq, and it has made a huge difference in my life. Your story is everyone's story. Everyone loves it. My wife uses it. Pennsylvania is over 150,000 cases of people using it now. Think about that's how many fewer people are using opioids or are self-medicating with alcohol and all of these things. It's crazy that we are still at a point where, you know, I could get up and smoke a carton of Marlboro, I could buy a dozen lottery tickets, I could go guzzle a bottle of Jack Daniels, I could go to a casino and gambling my life savings, you know, and I could do all of these things and it's perfectly legal even though it's deadly and destructive. But if I get caught with a joint, I'm going to be charged criminally. Why are we in this place? Why? That's a good question. Go ahead, Kim. Well, Anne, can you talk a little bit and I know you have it as about the economic impact that legal we would have on Pennsylvania with so many communities hurting and sort of looking for, you know, another economic engine, so to speak. Yeah, I just like, since when does any state have the to say, yeah, we don't need $5 billion. I mean, Oregon just topped a billion dollars in sales. We would generate $5 billion in revenue over the next 20 years, minimum, minimum. The auditor general released a figure somewhere like $550 million, let's say half of that, 20 years of half of that and you're at $5 billion in free money, free money that's going to the cartels, that's going to drug dealers. You know like think of what five billion dollars could do for the people of Pennsylvania because you know what? Every day we stay and keep our head in the sand. It's just go It's it's already here. It's not a matter of creating the market. It's just a matter of legalizing the market You know what, you mentioned previously about your optimism for the future. I happen to share some of that optimism, but I also think that because of this post-factual reality in which we live in the events that happened on the Capitol last week, that things could very well get worse before they get better. What would you say to folks facing maybe similar concerns? Well, I would just say that we're going to be OK. We're going to get through this. We absolutely are. And the guardrails held, people stepped up, did the right thing, and I'm optimistic. We have a vaccine that works, and we're rolling that out. And the vaccine is going to be like hand sanitizer. You know, you had people, you know, stabbing each other in the aisle and target over a bottle if you could find it in March. And now they have five gallon buckets for 1095 everywhere, you know? So we're going to get to a place this year where you can walk into a drug store and walk out with your COVID vaccine. We're going to get there. And we're going to put this nightmare behind us and we can get back and see each other more. We can see our grandparents, we can see our parents, we can have activities again. And I think that's going to alleviate a lot of the tension and a lot of this stuff too. And I'm looking forward to that. I think we're going to bounce back. I think there's reasons for optimism to come. You mentioned that. that rift between folks in the United States. Now, our previous great rift was during the Civil War, and that was a fight between the North and the South. But in the US, we see now a lot of the differences in opinion and consideration of what is fact is between rural and urban. And Pennsylvania is a great example of that. Because you go 30 miles outside of Pittsburgh and you're in the middle of farm country and in the mountains in some cases. How do we bridge that divide between urban and rural in America? Because that's where it seems to be growing wider and wider. We're going to have policies that benefit everybody. You know, one of the things that, you know, poor urban America has with poor rural Pennsylvania, dollar general stores, you know, you go to a poor community in rural Potter County and you go down the street and rank in Pennsylvania here and you're going to have dollar generals. I mean, there's a lot more in common than we have Not and there are people that foment the kind of stereotypes and the negativity that you see you know we call it pencil tucky or we call them rednecks or we you know whatever all these awful things that aren't true that aren't true and then the other side is is that you know it's you know dirty or, you know, just, you know, dog whistling about Philadelphia or Pittsburgh, all these terrible racist things too. And the truth is neither one of them are true. And there are outstanding examples, you know, in both camps. And that's one of the things that I would base my, I have based my candidacy on, and will base my candidacy on if I do get into the race, is this idea that we are a 67 county community here in Pennsylvania. And that we cannot move forward as a state if we pit rural versus urban. We can't. And I would be a senator for all 67 counties. And this understanding that there is a basket of things that make a lot of sense to rural Pennsylvania and urban Pennsylvania. We should advance those. Sounds good. Lieutenant Governor, thank you very much for your time. I really appreciate you joining us on the podcast and hope to have you back again sometime soon. Yeah, absolutely. Well, thank you so much for having me. It's a pleasure. And let me say, those were very thorough, thoughtful questions. So thank you for, I was actually a very stimulating conversation. So thank you for having me on. I want to thank Lieutenant Governor John Federman for joining me on the podcast. And I want to thank you for listening. These are fraught times, so be careful out there. Wear your mask, keep your distance, wash your hands, keep your eyes open. Keep your head on a swivel. Things are happening all around you. Don't get caught off guard. Thanks for listening.",
